## FAQ

**Q: What mechanical design software does Qmax Systems use?**
A: SOLIDWORKS, Creo Parametric, and Autodesk Inventor for 3D mechanical design. ANSYS and SolidWorks Simulation for structural and thermal analysis. Drawings issued per ISO 286 / ASME Y14.5 GD&T standards.

**Q: Can Qmax manage both the mechanical design and production sourcing?**
A: Yes. End-to-end: concept and CAD through drawing release, vendor qualification, prototype oversight, and production ramp. We work with your approved manufacturer list or introduce qualified vendors, coordinating DFM reviews, first-article inspection, and tolerance verification.

**Q: How does Qmax approach MIL-STD-810 mechanical qualification?**
A: FEA structural analysis (static stress, random vibration, shock, thermal) before any hardware is built, using MIL-STD-810H test profiles as the loading boundary. This identifies failure modes early, reduces prototype-test cycles, and produces the documentation trail required for DRDO/DGQA and US military qualification submissions.

**Q: What is the difference between IP65, IP67, and IP68, and how do you ensure compliance?**
A: IP65: dust-tight, low-pressure water jets. IP67: adds 1m immersion for 30 min. IP68: continuous immersion at specified depth. Compliance requires specifying correct O-ring cross-section, groove geometry, gasket material (silicone, EPDM, Viton), fastener torque, and surface flatness — validated at an accredited test facility.

**Q: Does Qmax provide GD&T drawings suitable for direct issue to vendors?**
A: Yes. Fully dimensioned GD&T-annotated drawings to ISO 286 / ASME Y14.5. Tolerances set based on functional requirements and manufacturing process. Any competent machine shop can manufacture without verbal clarification.

**Q: How does Qmax integrate mechanical and electronics design to avoid late-stage conflicts?**
A: Mechanical engineers work concurrently with PCB layout and firmware teams from the first concept review. Connector keepout zones, thermal vent placement, board mounting datum, and harness routing are resolved in CAD before layout begins.

**Q: What is the typical timeline from mechanical concept to qualified prototype?**
A: Simple plastic enclosure: 4–6 weeks. Sheet-metal rack chassis: 6–8 weeks. MIL-STD-810H qualified rugged aluminum enclosure: 12–18 weeks including FEA pre-qualification and first-article test.